# Normalize line endings: store text as LF, check out LF on every platform.
# Without this, core.autocrlf=true checks sources out as CRLF on Windows, so
# build.py concatenates a mixed-EOL deliverable that diverges from the LF blob
# (a phantom git-dirty diff + a fresh-clone build_integrity failure). eol=lf
# keeps the working tree LF so the build is byte-reproducible everywhere.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ...but the visual-regression baselines are BINARY, and `text=auto` above is free to GUESS.
# A PNG that gets EOL-normalised is a corrupt PNG -- and it would corrupt SILENTLY, on checkout,
# on somebody else's machine, surfacing as "the pixel check is flaky" rather than as what it is.
# Be explicit. (`binary` = `-text -diff`.)
*.png binary

# Same argument, same trap, a second file type. The W-X1 print wave commits real A4 page.pdf
# output as its review artifact (_audit/w16-print-before-after/), and a PDF carries FlateDecode
# streams -- arbitrary bytes, CR and LF among them. EOL-normalising one produces a file whose
# stream lengths no longer match its /Length entries: a corrupt PDF that git checks out silently
# on the next Windows clone. text=auto's heuristic is not a guarantee, so do not rely on it
# noticing.
*.pdf binary
